{"id":26,"course_id":7,"title":"Karpenter, NodePools, and Disruption Budgets","summary":"Use dynamic capacity without letting autoscaling surprise production.","sequence":2,"body":"## Advanced capacity\nKarpenter can provision nodes quickly based on pending Pods and consolidate underused nodes. In production, it needs constraints, disruption policy, and observability.\n- Define allowed instance families, zones, capacity types, and limits.\n- Separate system workloads from dynamic application capacity.\n- Respect PodDisruptionBudgets and topology spread constraints.\n- Watch consolidation, expire-after, interruption handling, and Spot behavior.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl get nodepool,nodeclaim\n$ kubectl get nodes -L karpenter.sh/nodepool,node.kubernetes.io/instance-type,topology.kubernetes.io/zone\n$ kubectl -n karpenter logs deploy/karpenter --since=30m\n$ kubectl get pdb -A\nLook for expensive oversized nodes, single-AZ placement, consolidation blocked by PDBs, and system Pods on risky capacity.\n\n## Tradeoff\nKarpenter improves right-sizing and speed, but unconstrained provisioning can increase cost or reduce availability during disruption.","practice_notes":"Scenario: Nodes scaled out for a batch job and never scaled in.\nChecklist:\n- Check whether Pods still request capacity.\n- Read Karpenter consolidation reasons.\n- Inspect PDBs and topology constraints.\n- Compare requested resources with actual usage.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get nodes --show-labels\n$ kubectl get pdb -A\n$ kubectl -n karpenter logs deploy/karpenter --since=1h","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-production-eks-architecture","course_category":"EKS","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":126,"term":"Karpenter","context":"Autoscaling","definition":"A Kubernetes-native node provisioning controller."},{"id":127,"term":"NodePool","context":"Capacity policy","definition":"A Karpenter object defining provisioning constraints and disruption policy."},{"id":128,"term":"NodeClaim","context":"Provisioned node","definition":"A Karpenter object representing a requested node."},{"id":129,"term":"Consolidation","context":"Cost control","definition":"Replacing or removing nodes to reduce waste while respecting constraints."},{"id":130,"term":"PodDisruptionBudget","context":"Availability","definition":"A policy limiting voluntary disruption for matching Pods."}],"flashcards":[{"id":76,"lesson_id":26,"prompt":"What does Karpenter react to?","answer":"Pending Pods and scheduling requirements.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":77,"lesson_id":26,"prompt":"What can block consolidation?","answer":"PDBs, topology constraints, required Pods, NodePool policy, or disruption settings.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":78,"lesson_id":26,"prompt":"Why constrain NodePools?","answer":"To control cost, availability zones, instance choices, and workload isolation.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
